{
	title: 'Terminal status row: session, cwd, model, context',
	spec: "Between the prompt box's bottom rule and the help row (task p0) the terminal draws one status row like the old Hal's renderStatusLine (artifact tasks/5p/old-render-status.ts). Left, joined with ' · ': the session id, then ': <name>' in the highlight color when the session has a name (e.g. '156-way: Orchestrate v3'); the cwd with the home shortened to ~ (the Hal dir in the assistant color, others highlighted); the model's display name (Opus 5.5); the context used, e.g. '87k/1000k (9%)', where used is the last round's input tokens (input + cache read + cache write) and max is the model's context window, both numbers heat-colored by the percentage. Right-aligned, joined with ' · ': this process's role (host or peer); the session's total tokens this run, '↑252 ↓41k' (sent and received, compact counts: 252, 4.1k, 41k, 1.2M); for a subscription account, 'Sub i/n: 5h 18%, 7d 57%' from task 61's usage windows (i/n only with more than one account), percentages heat-colored. When the row is too narrow, drop the right parts from the end (Sub, then tokens, then role), then clip the left. The host sends clients what they cannot know (context window, usage, usage windows) as part of session or host state it already pushes; nothing polls. The web shows the same facts in its own layout later; not required here. Tests: fitting at several widths drops parts in that order; the context percentage; the compact token counts; numbers update after a turn ends.",
